\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 Folders:\

\f1\b0\fs24 \
/bin: basic, essential commands live that anyone on computer is allowed to use.\
/dev: linux treats everything as a file.This folder is where the system communicates with physical hardware.\
/proc: it\'92s like a live dashboard, it shows realtime information as what\'92s the computer brain is doing \'93right now\'94\
/home: every user gets a private folder here to store their own personal files.\
/usr: big folder where bulk of extra software, apps or shared files are installed.\
/mnt: empty temporary folders that comp admin use as a temporary workspace to manually plug in drives.\
\

\f0\b\fs28 Commands:  \
\

\f1\b0\fs24 mount     \{lists every single drive or plugged in USB\}\
mount | wc -l    \{counts that list and gives a single number, eg - 10 drives are plugged in\}\
df -h     \{checks storage space, shows how much space is used and how much is free\}\
mount -t temps \'85       \{create a fake, temporary storage drive using comp\'92s RAM\}\
umount      \{eject command to disconnect a drive\} \
\
\

\f0\b\fs28 System files:
\f1\b0\fs24 \
\
tmpfs   \{not exactly a file but a type of temporary whiteboard memory\}\
/proc/cpuinfo   \{a file that tells you the exact specs of comp\'92s processor\}\
/proc/meminfo   \{a file that tells you how much RAM comp\'92s has and how much is being used}